::GEO_U03_L02_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
A triangle has angles 48° and 67°. What is the third angle?
{
~A. 55°#This subtracts incorrectly from 180°.
=B. 65°#48 + 67 = 115, and 180 - 115 = 65.
~C. 75°#These angles would total 190°.
~D. 115°#This is the sum of the two given angles, not the missing angle.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
A triangle has angles 2x°, 3x°, and 4x°. What is x?
{
~A. 18#This makes the sum 162°, not 180°.
~B. 30#This makes the sum 270°.
~C. 45#This makes the sum 405°.
=D. 20#2x + 3x + 4x = 180, so 9x = 180 and x = 20.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
An exterior angle is 110°. One remote interior angle is 45°. What is the other remote interior angle?
{
=A. 65°#By the Exterior Angle Theorem, 45 + x = 110, so x = 65.
~B. 55°#45 + 55 does not equal 110.
~C. 70°#45 + 70 equals 115, not 110.
~D. 155°#This adds instead of subtracting.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
Which theorem explains why triangle interior angles add to 180°?
{
~A. Exterior Angle Theorem#This theorem relates an exterior angle to two remote interior angles.
~B. Vertical Angles Theorem#Vertical angles are formed by intersecting lines.
=C. Triangle Angle Sum Theorem#This theorem states that the interior angles of a triangle sum to 180°.
~D. Pythagorean Theorem#The Pythagorean Theorem relates side lengths in right triangles.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
Which angles are remote interior angles for an exterior angle?
{
~A. The exterior angle and its adjacent interior angle.#The adjacent interior angle is not remote.
=B. The two interior angles not adjacent to the exterior angle.#Remote interior angles are the two nonadjacent interior angles.
~C. Only the largest angle.#Remote status depends on position, not size.
~D. Any two angles that look equal.#Appearance does not define remote interior angles.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
A triangle has angles x°, (x + 20)°, and 70°. What is x?
{
=A. 45#x + x + 20 + 70 = 180, so 2x = 90 and x = 45.
~B. 35#This makes the total 160°.
~C. 55#This makes the total 200°.
~D. 90#This treats x as the whole missing total.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
Remote interior angles are 38° and 72°. What is the exterior angle?
{
~A. 100°#The sum is 110°, not 100°.
~B. 34°#This subtracts instead of adding.
~C. 180°#180° is not the exterior angle here.
=D. 110°#The exterior angle equals 38 + 72 = 110°.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
A triangle has angles 60°, 60°, and 60°. What relationship is shown?
{
~A. The exterior angle equals 60°.#The item gives interior angles, not an exterior angle.
~B. The angle sum is 120°.#The sum is 180°, not 120°.
=C. The angles satisfy the Triangle Angle Sum Theorem.#60 + 60 + 60 = 180, matching the theorem.
~D. One angle is obtuse.#No angle is greater than 90°.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
A triangle has angles 33°, 82°, and x°. Which equation is correct?
{
=A. 33 + 82 + x \= 180#The three interior angles sum to 180°.
~B. 33 + 82 \= x#This makes x the sum of two known angles.
~C. 33 + x \= 82#This ignores the 180° total.
~D. 180 + x \= 33 + 82#This places 180° on the wrong side.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
An exterior angle is 130°. What is true about the two remote interior angles together?
{
~A. They sum to 50°.#50° is the adjacent interior angle in the linear pair.
~B. They sum to 180°.#180° is the total of all interior angles.
~C. They are each 130°.#The theorem gives a sum, not each angle.
=D. They sum to 130°.#The Exterior Angle Theorem says the remote interior angles sum to the exterior angle.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q11::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
A triangle has angles 74° and 39°. What is the missing angle?
{
~A. 57°#This does not make the total 180°.
=B. 67°#74 + 39 = 113 and 180 - 113 = 67.
~C. 77°#This makes the total 190°.
~D. 113°#This is the sum of the given angles.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q12::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
A triangle has angles x°, x°, and 80°. What is x?
{
~A. 40#This gives a total of 160°.
~B. 80#This gives a total of 240°.
=C. 50#2x + 80 = 180, so 2x = 100 and x = 50.
~D. 100#This gives a total of 280°.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q13::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
An exterior angle is 142°. One remote interior angle is 68°. Find the other remote interior angle.
{
~A. 64°#68 + 64 = 132, not 142.
~B. 82°#68 + 82 = 150, not 142.
~C. 210°#This adds the exterior angle and remote angle.
=D. 74°#68 + x = 142, so x = 74.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q14::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
A triangle has angles (x + 10)°, (x + 30)°, and 80°. What is x?
{
=A. 30#x + 10 + x + 30 + 80 = 180, so 2x + 120 = 180 and x = 30.
~B. 20#This makes the angle sum 160°.
~C. 40#This makes the angle sum 200°.
~D. 60#This makes the angle sum 240°.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q15::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
An exterior angle and its adjacent interior angle form what total?
{
~A. 90°#90° only applies if the pair is specifically complementary, which is not given.
~B. The exterior angle only#The adjacent interior angle must be included.
=C. 180°#They form a linear pair, so their measures sum to 180°.
~D. The two remote interior angles only#Remote interior angles relate to the exterior angle, not the adjacent linear-pair total.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q16::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
If an exterior angle is 118°, what is the adjacent interior angle?
{
~A. 72°#72° does not complete the linear pair to 180°.
=B. 62°#The exterior and adjacent interior angle form a linear pair: 180 - 118 = 62.
~C. 118°#Adjacent and exterior angles are not generally equal.
~D. 180°#180° is the total, not the adjacent angle.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q17::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
A triangle has interior angles 2x°, (x + 15)°, and 75°. What is x?
{
~A. 25#This gives a total of 165°.
~B. 35#This gives a total of 195°.
=C. 30#2x + x + 15 + 75 = 180, so 3x + 90 = 180 and x = 30.
~D. 45#This gives a total of 225°.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q18::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
An exterior angle is 96°. The remote interior angles are x° and 2x°. What is x?
{
=A. 32#x + 2x = 96, so 3x = 96 and x = 32.
~B. 24#This gives a remote interior sum of 72°.
~C. 48#This gives a remote interior sum of 144°.
~D. 64#This gives a remote interior sum of 192°.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q19::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
Which statement correctly uses the Exterior Angle Theorem?
{
~A. Exterior angle \= adjacent interior angle#The adjacent interior angle forms a linear pair with the exterior angle.
~B. Exterior angle \= sum of all three interior angles#All three interior angles sum to 180°, not the exterior angle.
~C. Exterior angle \= half of a remote interior angle#The theorem does not use half of a remote angle.
=D. Exterior angle \= sum of two remote interior angles#Correct. This is the Exterior Angle Theorem.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q20::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
A triangle has two angles measuring 41° and 99°. What is the third angle?
{
~A. 50°#This makes the total 190°.
=B. 40°#41 + 99 = 140, and 180 - 140 = 40.
~C. 58°#This makes the total 198°.
~D. 140°#This is the sum of the known angles.
}
::GEO_U03_L02_Q21::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
Which first step best supports this lesson skill?
{
~A. Guess from the picture before checking labels.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=B. Identify the givens and the geometric object before choosing a relationship.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Triangle Angle Relationships.
~C. Skip directly to the final answer.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~D. Use a formula that is not connected to the given information.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
